i do not have any ac units unfortunately  i sold out of those within the first two  days i brought them in this week  i didn't think it would be this hot i  mean i come here all the time but  today is just like really the surface of  the ground is super hot  we just have the air ceiling the the  fans yeah the fans we have fans and  that's all we just keep the  the windows open and we try to have that  a cross air you know like  makes it cool everything yeah yeah that  works really well and it saves a lot of  energy  yeah i think that we all should be doing  our part to save  as much energy conserve as much as we  can  yeah it may be the first day of summer  in the northern hemisphere but for some  50 million people in the u.s  summer has arrived early and hotter than  ever before in just the last week  high temperature records have been  shattered all across the western half  of the united states let's pull in the  map to show you what we're talking about  salt lake city in utah just saw its  hottest day  since record keeping began back in 1870  107 degrees fahrenheit that's 42 degrees  celsius you see it right there wyoming  also saw  new records in nevada las vegas  continues to flirt with its all-time  high  of 47 degrees celsius you see right  right there but the u.s city melting the  most  phoenix arizona which just set an  all-time record of five consecutive days  of 115 degrees fahrenheit you see it  right there that's  46 degrees celsius now the heat  is making severe droughts across the  western u.s go from bad to worse the  federal government is already planning  to declare  an official water shortage at lake mead  in august  lake mead's waters power hoover dam now  this picture  it tells the story doesn't it as of last  week lake mead's water level is at a  record low and there is no relief in  sight  the western u.s is in what scientists  are describing now as a climate  change-induced  mega-drought some even say that this  summer could be the hottest and the  driest in the millennium  and less water means more fire  2020 saw a record number of wildfires in  california oregon  and in washington state 2021 is expected  to be  worse stephen pine is a leading  environmental scientist who has written  extensively on fire and climate change i  want you to take a listen  to how he describes what is happening  i think what we're seeing with climate  change is that it is  acting on and exaggerating all kinds of  climatic  conditions and so it is making things  worse we're seeing  more frequent fires more severe fires  uh we're seeing them at a scale not just  in terms of area largeness but intensity  plus area combining i've got children  i've got grandchildren  what kind of world are they going to  inhabit it's going to be a very  different one  if the climate change trajectory isn't  flattened  then all those will be overridden i mean  all those  temporizing things all those small scale  things we do  will simply be overwhelmed my next guest  has also been sounding the alarm on the  increasing number of weather  catastrophes compounded by  climate change rachel cletus is with the  union of concerned scientists she joins  me tonight  from arlington massachusetts rachel it's  good to have you on  the program last year you testified  before a u.s  house committee and you warned of heat  waves droughts  and wildfires becoming more extreme and  more common  and that is exactly what is happening  now before our very eyes isn't it  yes absolutely it's very very sobering  to see  climate changes here and now this is not  about some distant future  we're seeing this terrible heat wave  that you just described on your program  unfolding around the western u.s right  now  we're seeing conditions of extreme  drought  setting up for a terrible wildfire  season that's already begun  it's already underway in the western u.s  and  we're just now in june so this is just  the front end of summer  very sobering rachel can you point to  any real  action or tangible measures of the u.s  government since you testified  last year before that committee  yeah so just to unpack it a little uh we  are here in this moment this terrible  drought and wildfire season  not because of any individual year this  is a multi-year trend that has been set  up  climate change is driving these very  extreme  high temperatures we're seeing earlier  snowpack melt  we're seeing the ground very very dry  in a multi-year fashion and that's  setting up a wildfire season of the kind  we're describing  we have a lot of catch-up to do and  that's just the bottom line so this  uh administration the new administration  that's come in this year  as well as congress really has a lot of  ground to make up  we have to be both investing in making  sure that our forests are healthy for us  ecosystems are healthy  that we're investing of course in the  emergency fire fighting and the relief  measures that communities need  but we have to be thinking about the  long-term trend here  we're not going to be able to avoid all  of these conditions  uh the fact is our current and past heat  trapping emissions  have already created really dangerous  conditions  so now the question is how can we try to  minimize the risk  i know as someone who studies the  climate you described  the election of president biden in  november like  waking up from a nightmare he  immediately rejoined the paris climate  agreement that is true but other than  that the world has been focused on his  infrastructure bill racial equity and  just recently on the transatlantic  alliances and  u.s russia relations have we overlooked  important climate change measures  look climate is connected to everything  it's connected to our economic  well-being it's connected to our future  prosperity  it's connected to our infrastructure  decisions because what we're finding is  when we fail to take climate risks into  account  our infrastructure is starting to fail  more and more  we're talking about wildfires for  example and last year we saw the power  grid in california failed repeatedly  in the face of these wildfire risks we  were seeing  utility companies take proactive  measures to shut down  power during high wildfire risk moments  and that left a lot of people these  rolling blackouts that have other  cascading impacts  or we saw the power grid itself trip  uh wildfires so we have to  any infrastructure we're investing in  right now as part of this american jobs  plan that president biden has outlined  needs to be both low carbon and climate  resilient to prepare for the future  that's really coming our way right now  you talked for example about the power  grid  and the connections with the low  water supply and we're seeing these  we're seeing hydropower being  reduced the access to hydropower because  of the low water levels in these  reservoirs  all of these conditions are impacting  our infrastructure and therefore we need  to be taking proactive measures to make  them more climate resilient going  forward  and rachel let me ask you how do you  grade the way  we the media are reporting on  whether extremes and climate change  well i think more and more we're seeing  reporting that's connecting the dots  between climate change and these  extreme disasters but probably not  enough and people are still looking at  them as one-off disasters instead of the  terrible trend that we've set up we have  a wildfire season right now that's  getting underway but we also here in the  united states have an above average  hurricane season that's forecasted  we're going to see these kinds of risks  collide and government agencies like in  the us the federal emergency management  agency are going to be tasked with  dealing  with multiple disasters at the same time  we're also seeing communities being hit  hard repeatedly  communities that were hit by wildfires  last year are still  struggling to get back on their feet  similarly with hurricane affected  communities in the gulf south and  the coastal u.s so this is where  we have a situation where dealing with  these as one-off disasters while they're  in the headlines is just not enough  we have to take proactive resilience  measures  you know we throw around these record  high temperatures all the time and we  assume they mean something to our  viewers  scientists in australia they're before  referring more and more instead  to what is known as the excess heat  factor which  factors in how much impact and stress a  heat wave has on people for example  in your opinion is that a better gauge  to use to communicate what climate  change is doing to our weather  and doing to us  yes we need some way to convey the truly  exceptional conditions that we have when  we have these  multi-day events of extreme heat because  you don't get that cooling effect at  night times  uh you get the human body being really  stressed especially people who are  working outdoors  like right now workers are working in  the fields  in the western us construction workers  even children who are doing sports  outdoors when you have these multi-day  heat events  there is no break and it is truly  catastrophic for the public health  for homeless for the incarcerated  population  and that's why we need a way of  conveying to the public the true risks  that they face to be really careful of  course to stay indoors and stay cool as  far as possible  but also some of these places are going  to get really unhealthy for people to  live in when we  look out into the future and that is  uh when we recognize that we have to  curtail the emissions that are fueling  climate change  there's a limit to what we can adapt to  do you still believe rachel that we can  save  the world in the climate as we know it  if we cut carbon emissions to zero no  later  than by 2050 is it still possible  look even at one degree celsius global  average temperature increase which we're  at right now a little over that we're  seeing some really profound effects  but yes we do still have a very  consequential  choice in terms of limiting how far and  how bad these impacts get we have to do  everything in our power to limit those  emissions  otherwise we will get really  catastrophic conditions  so we can't avoid all the harms but we  can limit some of the worst harms and we  should  and i have to ask you about we've still  got you here a carbon tax  on everything that that we buy services  everything that creates co2 emissions  would that be the most equitable way  to get rid of co2 emissions and to make  sure that we're all invested in that  a carbon tax is not a silver bullet  solution it can definitely be an  important powerful  tool but when we're thinking about  equity and justice we really have to be  thinking about  who's being hurt hardest and at the  foremost from these impacts and making  sure that they're protected that they  have the investments  in their communities right rachel cletus  with the union of concerned scientists  rachel it's good having you on the  program we appreciate your time and your  insights tonight please come back and  talk with us again thank you  thank you very much for having me

